course: everyday
lesson: 102
title: Gratitude, helping at home and privacy
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Saying thank you shows people that you noticed their kindness and value it, and thinking of good things each day makes you happier too.
explain: A family is a team: when everyone helps a little, by tidying, setting the table or folding laundry, the work is quicker and nobody is worn out.
explain: Everyone also deserves privacy: their own room, their diary, their messages and their time in the bathroom.
explain: For example, a girl in Dublin knocks on her brother's door and waits for «come in», just as she would like him to do for her.
fact: Saying thank you, and maybe writing a card, shows you value a person's kindness.
fact: Naming three good things from your day before bed helps you sleep and feel happier.
fact: Thanking bus drivers, shop staff and dinner staff makes their day better.
fact: Putting things away where they belong every day keeps a room easy to clean and things easy to find.
fact: Setting the table and folding laundry are small jobs that save time for everyone.
fact: When washing up, ask for help with sharp knives and very hot water, because knives can cut and hot water can scald, or burn, your skin.
fact: Offering a small job to a tired parent means a lot.
fact: Families agree together which chores are paid and which are just part of family life.
fact: Knock on a closed door and wait for an answer before going in.
fact: Never read someone else's diary or messages without permission, because it breaks trust.
fact: If someone reads your diary, tell them it hurt you and ask an adult to help keep it private.
fact: Close the bathroom door, and expect others to knock, because toilet time is private.
fact: Ask before telling people private family news.
exercise: Your grandma sends you a sweater for your birthday. What is a kind thing to do? | Say thank you and maybe write a card | Say nothing because it is not a toy | Tell her you wanted something else | Thank her only if you like it
exercise: What is a good habit before going to sleep? | Thinking of three good things from your day | Going over everything that went wrong | Playing an exciting tablet game in bed | Reading scary stories about monsters
exercise: The bus driver drops you off at school. What is polite? | Say thank you as you get off | Say nothing, as it is their job | Thank only the drivers you know | Wave at the other children instead
exercise: What keeps your bedroom easy to clean? | Putting things away where they belong each day | Tidying it only once a month in one go | Pushing everything under the bed | Leaving it for a parent to tidy
exercise: Dinner is almost ready. How can you help? | Set out plates, forks, knives and glasses for everyone | Sit down at the table and wait for the food to come | Set only your own place | Turn on the TV so everyone can watch it
exercise: You are helping with the washing up and there is a big sharp knife. What do you do? | Ask a grown-up to wash the knife | Wash it fast before anyone sees | Leave it in the soapy water | Wipe it along your hand to dry it
exercise: How can you help with the clean laundry? | Match the socks and fold clothes neatly | Throw it all in a drawer | Wear the clean clothes straight away | Put it all back in the laundry basket to be washed again
exercise: Your dad comes home very tired from work. What is a kind thing to do? | Offer to help with a small job | Ask him to play a game right now | Tell him about all your problems | Ask him to drive you to a friend's
exercise: You want money for every chore you do. What is fair? | Agree with your parents which chores are paid | Refuse to help unless you are paid | Charge your parents for setting the table every single day | Do chores only for neighbours who pay
exercise: Your sister's bedroom door is closed. What should you do? | Knock and wait for an answer | Walk straight in | Knock and walk in at once | Look through the keyhole first
exercise: You find your brother's diary open on his bed. What do you do? | Leave it without reading it | Read just one page | Read it and keep the secrets | Show it to your friends
exercise: You find out your cousin read your diary. What should you do? | Tell him it hurt you and ask an adult for help keeping it private | Read his diary in secret to get even with him | Throw his things away | Never speak to him again, not even when he says sorry
exercise: Someone is in the bathroom and the door is closed. What do you do? | Knock and wait | Open the door to check | Shout at them to hurry | Wait outside with the door open
exercise: Your parents told you that your mum is changing jobs. A friend asks what is new at home. What is wise? | Ask your parents before telling people private family news | Tell everyone at school straight away | Make up a different story | Post it online for everyone
test: Which is the main reason to thank the dinner staff at school? | It makes them feel valued and their day better | You get bigger portions at lunch if you thank them | It is a rule that is checked | They will tell your teacher if you do not
test: Why is it a problem to read someone's messages without asking? | They are private and it breaks trust | Messages are always boring | It uses up their phone battery | You might learn something useful about them that way
test: Noah's mum is ill in bed. Which is the best way for Noah to help? | Do a small job like setting the table | Ask his mum for a lift to a friend's | Play loud music to cheer her up | Leave all his toys on the stairs
test: Which is true about helping at home? | Some jobs are simply part of family life | Every chore should be paid for in pocket money | Only adults should do housework | Children help only on birthdays
test: Why should you ask a grown-up to help with very hot washing-up water? | Hot water can scald your skin | Hot water cleans dishes too much | Hot water uses up all the soap | Grown-ups like hot water better
test: Your little brother walks into your room without knocking. What is a good way to deal with it? | Kindly ask him to knock and wait next time | Lock him out of the house | Barge into his room to show how it feels | Shout at him until he cries
test: Grace writes a thank-you card to her teacher at the end of the year. Why is that a good idea? | It shows she valued the teacher's kindness | Teachers must get a card from each child in the class | It will change her final marks | It is the only way to say goodbye
test: Why put your shoes away in the same place every day? | You can find them quickly when you need them | Shoes wear out if they are left out | It makes them grow bigger | Parents will buy you new ones every time if you do
test: found my sisters diary lol, gonna have a read | No, reading it without permission breaks trust | Yes, if she never finds out it's fine to read it | Yes, if it was left open | Yes, sisters share everything
test: mums wiped out after work, anything i can do | Offer a small job like setting the table | Ask her to make you a snack | Turn the TV up loud for her | Leave her a list of all the things you want for tea
test: bus driver dropped us at the stop, say anything? | Say thank you to the driver | Nothing, it is his job | Only thank him if he was early | Wave at him without a word
test: door's shut, i just walk in right, its my brother's room | Knock and wait for an answer first | Just walk in, it is your house too after all | Open it slowly so he knows | Knock while you walk in
explain: A girl in California posted a photo with her school uniform and street sign behind her; a stranger online worked out where she lived and messaged her, and her parents had to report it.
explain: A boy in Leeds left toys on the stairs after being asked to tidy up; his grandad tripped on one and broke his wrist.
fact: Photos that show your school, street or house number can let strangers find where you live.
fact: Things left on stairs are a common cause of falls at home, especially for older people.
test: posting a selfie in my uniform outside our house | Don't, strangers can work out where you live | Post it, nobody ever looks at those | Post it but smile less | Only post it at night
test: cba to tidy, leaving my toys on the stairs | Tidy them, someone could trip and fall down the stairs | Leave them, people always look where they're walking on stairs | Only big toys are a problem | Put them on every other step
test: someone asked my address n school in a game chat | Don't tell them, tell a trusted adult | Tell them, it's just a game so it doesn't matter | Tell only the street name | Give a fake one and keep chatting
test: did u hear Lily's mum read her diary n told her aunties what was in it, now Lily won't speak to her lol. what's the real lesson | Reading someone's diary breaks trust, so ask permission | Parents may read their children's diaries and share them with anyone they like | Diaries should be read aloud so nobody has secrets | Lily is wrong to be upset about her own diary
test: someone said a girl posted a pic in her uniform n a stranger found her house that same night n broke in, my nan read it somewhere. what's the true part | Photos showing school or street can let strangers find you | Every photo online leads to a break-in that night | Only uniforms in photos can show where you live | None of it, since photos online can never show anyone where a person lives
test: Harry's grandad tripped on his Lego on the stairs n broke his wrist, Harry's getting it so bad lol, shall i tell the group chat? | No, it is private family news; the lesson is to keep stairs clear | Yes, because everyone should laugh at Harry | Yes, but only if you add that Harry left the Lego there on purpose to trip him | Yes, so Harry's grandad knows people are talking
exam: Identify the principal reason for which one knocks upon a closed door and awaits a reply before entering. | Every person is entitled to privacy within his own room | Knocking ensures that the room is unoccupied | Doors are damaged over time when they are opened without knocking first | It is required only when visiting a neighbour
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning objects left upon a staircase. | They are a common cause of falls, especially for older persons | They present no hazard provided the staircase is lit | They are hazardous only to persons who are descending the stairs quickly | They present a hazard only to small children
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the naming of three good things from the day before sleep? | It assists sleep and increases a person's happiness | It is required of children by most schools | It diminishes happiness by recalling the events of the day | It is of benefit only to persons of advanced age
exam: Pursuant to the publication of a photograph online, ascertain which feature ought to be excluded so as to preserve safety. | The school, the street or the number of the house | The colour of the clothing worn by the person | The season of the year in which it was taken | The facial expression of the person shown
